  "winwsScopeWarning": "Change TCP traffic for all apps?",
  "winwsRequirements": "This VPN mode is unavailable",
  "emptyServersTitle": "Add a server or a subscription",
  "emptyServersBody": "Paste a subscription link from your VPN service, add your own server or scan a QR code.",
  "qrShort": "QR code",
  "extAboutHello": "Example panel: shows active connections and sends a test notification.",
  "extAboutZapret": "Bypasses blocking without a VPN by reshaping the start of connections so the provider cannot recognise the site.",
  "extAboutByedpi": "Bypasses blocking without a VPN through the local ByeDPI proxy.",
  "modeTunHint": "All of the computer's traffic goes through the VPN",
  "modeProxyHint": "Only browsers and apps that use the system proxy",
  "modeBypassHint": "Bypass blocking without a VPN server",
  "modeExtensionHint": "Blocking bypass from an extension",
  "engineInfo": "The engine is the program inside Sora that connects to a server and carries your traffic through it. Different engines understand different protocols and behave differently on poor networks.\n\nIn Auto mode Sora picks the engine that fits your server, and usually there is nothing else to do. Xray works best with VLESS, REALITY and XHTTP. sing-box is fast and understands many protocols. mihomo is handy for Clash subscriptions and complex rules.\n\nChange the engine by hand if a server will not connect or keeps dropping. You can always switch back.",
  "tunStackInfo": "In TUN mode Sora creates a virtual network card, and all of the computer's traffic goes through it. The stack decides what reads those packets.\n\nSystem uses the operating system's own network stack and is usually the fastest. gVisor reads packets inside the engine: a little slower, but more compatible with apps and firewalls. Mixed sends TCP through the system and UDP through gVisor.\n\nIf everything works, leave it alone. Try another stack if sites load badly or games and calls lag.",
  "protection": "Protection",
  "killSwitchInfo": "Normally, when a VPN drops, apps carry on directly and your provider sees your real traffic. This setting prevents that: while the VPN is on or recovering, the internet goes through it and nowhere else.\n\nIf the connection breaks, sites simply stop loading until it reconnects. Your local network, such as the router and printer, keeps working.\n\nWhen you turn the VPN off yourself, the block lifts and the internet works as usual.",
  "strictKillSwitchInfo": "Strict mode goes further: the block stays even if you close Sora, turn the VPN off or restart the computer. Without a VPN there is no internet at all until you turn this switch off.\n\nIt is for people who need to be sure not a single packet leaves outside the VPN, even if the app fails.\n\nFor everyday use, the regular setting is enough. If you turned this on and lost the network, come back here and turn it off.",
  "zapretWithVpnInfo": "Not all traffic has to go through a VPN. Rules can send some sites directly, for example local services that do not work from abroad. If your provider slows or blocks such a site, the VPN cannot help, because that traffic does not pass through it.\n\nThis switch sends direct traffic through Zapret, which reshapes the start of each connection so the provider's filter does not recognise the site. Traffic that goes through the VPN stays as it is.\n\nIf all of your traffic goes through the VPN, this makes no difference.",
  "failoverInfo": "When you connect to a group of servers, Sora watches whether the current one answers. If it stops, the connection moves to the next working server in the group on its own.\n\nWhile the server changes, the connection may drop for a few seconds.\n\nTurn this off to always stay on one server.",
  "ipv6Info": "IPv6 is the newer version of internet addresses. Not every provider or VPN server supports it.\n\nWith this on, IPv6 traffic also goes through the VPN. If the server does not support IPv6, sites may load slowly.\n\nWith it off, Sora uses IPv4 only, which is more reliable. Turn it on if you know your server works with IPv6.",
  "fragmentInfo": "When an app opens a secure connection, the server name goes in plain text at the very start. This is called SNI, and it tells your provider's filter where you are connecting.\n\nFragmentation splits that start into small pieces. The server puts them back together, while a simple filter cannot, so the connection gets through.\n\nThis applies to the connection to your VPN server. Turn it on if a server will not connect although it works for others: that often means the provider is blocking the connection to it.",
  "splitPosInfo": "The bypass works by cutting the first packet of a connection into parts. This sets where to cut.\n\nYou can write a byte number, such as 1 or 2, or a marker. The most useful marker is midsld, the middle of the domain: the site name ends up torn across two packets. Separate several places with commas, for example 1,midsld.\n\nThe defaults are usually enough. Change them if the bypass does not work: different providers need different values.",
  "disorderInfo": "The first packet is cut into parts, which normally go in order. With this on, the second part goes first.\n\nThe site still gets the data correctly, because TCP puts the parts back in place. Your provider's filter often looks only at the first piece to arrive and misses the site name.\n\nTurn it on if plain splitting is not enough. On some networks it slows pages a little.",
  "tlsRecordInfo": "Data inside TLS is packed into records. The start of a connection normally sits in one record, where a filter can easily find the site name.\n\nThis setting splits it into two records. The server reads both, while filters that check only the first lose the site name.\n\nIt works well together with packet splitting. A few old servers may refuse it: if a site stops opening, turn this off.",
  "hostCaseInfo": "On sites without HTTPS, the request goes in plain text, including the Host header with the site name. Simple filters look for its exact spelling.\n\nThis setting changes the letters of the header name: Host becomes hoSt. To the server it is the same, but an old filter no longer recognises the request.\n\nIt has no effect on HTTPS. Few sites work without it today, so this is rarely needed.",
  "probeMethodInfo": "The numbers next to servers show how quickly each one answers. There are several ways to measure that.\n\nThrough the engine: a real request to a site through the server. The most honest method, it shows whether the VPN really works, but it takes longer.\nConnection: only connects to the server's port. Fast, but does not check that the VPN works.\nTLS handshake: connects and starts encryption with the server.\nICMP: a plain ping. Some working servers do not answer it.\n\nAuto uses the engine, and a plain connection where it cannot. That is enough for most people.",
  "autoSelectInfo": "How Fastest picks a server: by ping, by measured speed or by both.",
  "autoSwitchInfo": "After each ping check, Sora looks at the chosen server. If it has become much slower or stopped answering and a faster one is available, Sora switches on its own.\n\nThe switch takes a couple of seconds and happens at most once every 10 minutes, so the connection does not jump around.\n\nThis only affects a server you picked by hand: Fastest already chooses by itself.",
  "probeTls": "TLS handshake",
  "probeIcmp": "ICMP (ping)",
  "serverConfig": "Server config",
  "copy": "Copy",
  "profilesEnabled": "Use profiles",
  "profilesEnabledInfo": "A network profile remembers settings for a specific network: one server at home, another at work.\n\nWhile this switch is off, profiles do nothing: Sora changes no settings and sends no notices about networks.\n\nOnce it is on, Sora recognises a network by its Wi-Fi name or router address and offers the profile's settings. If a profile allows it, Sora applies them without asking.",
  "extensionBypassInfo": "This is a way around blocks without a VPN. The extension reshapes the start of each connection so the provider's filter does not recognise the site, and the page opens directly. Your IP address stays the same.\n\nIt runs instead of a VPN: turning it on ends the current connection, and turning it off returns Sora to the server you had chosen.\n\nIt does not suit everyone: different providers need different profiles, and sites blocked by IP address will not open this way. You choose the profile in the extension's panel.",
  "infoSources": "Read more",
  "extFilter": "Filter",
  "extBypassRun": "Blocking bypass",
  "chainNoServers": "Only regular servers can join a chain. Xray profiles run on their own, so they are not listed here."
